---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/shopify-email-vs-attentive';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/shopify-email-vs-attentive');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/shopify-email-vs-attentive');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Shopify Email limited to email recovery?",
    "answer": "This route uses the familiar name, but current Shopify Messaging documentation includes email, SMS and WhatsApp automation. Check channel availability in your store before comparing it with Attentive."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Shopify and Attentive run recovery together?",
    "answer": "Only with an explicit owner for each stage and channel and tested exclusions. Internal Attentive stage precedence does not establish suppression of Shopify automations."
  },
  {
    "question": "What is the correct discount calculation?",
    "answer": "In the hypothetical $368 basket, fixed product cost is $213.44. After a 15% discount, revenue is $312.80 and gross profit is $99.36 before other variable costs."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should the Attentive quote include?",
    "answer": "Specify required journey access, email and text volume, destination mix, onboarding and services. Compare the added cost with incremental gross profit after variable costs, not attributed revenue alone."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Shopify Email vs Attentive for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">Decide whether native recovery is enough or a dedicated email and SMS program earns its extra operating cost.</p>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">

<h2>Start with the native baseline that exists today</h2>
<p>This route retains the familiar Shopify Email name, but Shopify now documents recovery in Shopify Messaging. Its automation editor includes email, SMS and WhatsApp. Availability and eligibility must be checked in your store; buying Attentive solely to “add texts” skips that native comparison. Native abandoned-checkout email is excluded from the email send allowance, while SMS and WhatsApp are charged by recipient region. <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations/create">Shopify automation setup</a> explains the channels and charging distinction.</p>
<p>Choose the native route when your team can express the intended recovery in its available templates and Flow, and can own the audience, content and reporting in Shopify. A failing payment or unaffordable shipping charge needs a checkout or service fix before another reminder. A second platform does not repair those causes.</p>
<h2>What Attentive adds to the decision</h2>
<p>Attentive documents separate browse, cart and checkout abandonment journeys, with precedence for the latest stage, plus combined text and email templates. Its <a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/4410320387732-Email-abandonment-journeys">abandonment guide</a> supports a more deliberate multichannel program. That is useful when the same shopper moves between stages and the team needs differentiated content rather than another identical checkout reminder.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/360041854231-Shopify">Shopify integration guide</a> describes the tag's role in sign-up units and site events. Make identity capture part of the implementation budget: a shopper adding an item is not proof that either channel can reach them. Ask for a demonstration using your theme, consent tools and target markets. Trigger access can depend on plan, so the quote must name the journeys you intend to use.</p>
<h2>Choose one recovery owner per stage and channel</h2>
<p>Write a small ownership map before connecting anything: browse email, cart email, checkout email and recovery text. For each, name Shopify or Attentive and identify the other automation to pause. Attentive's internal stage precedence is not evidence that a native Shopify automation will be suppressed. If Shopify keeps checkout email while Attentive handles texts, the team still needs a shared rule for purchases, opt-outs and overlapping campaigns.</p>
<p>Do not copy a phone number into a text audience merely because it exists on an order. Retain channel permission history and exclusions when moving audiences. Confirm the applicable account and market rules with the supplier; the product documentation is not a substitute for assessing your permission records.</p>
<h2>Proposed acceptance test: stage progression and duplicate sends</h2>
<ol><li>With an authorized test contact, view an item, add it, then start checkout. Record event times and the journey selected at each stage. Confirm that older-stage messages stop as intended.</li><li>Repeat with email-only eligibility and with eligibility for both channels. Confirm the actual branch, sender and destination rather than relying on a diagram.</li><li>Complete a purchase while a reminder is waiting. Inspect both platforms for cancellation and check that no other recovery flow sends the same offer.</li><li>Opt out, then repeat a site visit. Check suppression and retain the send or exclusion logs. Test the return link on mobile with the intended variant and discount.</li></ol>
<p>These are proposed tests, not results from an installed store. Pass only when the observed events and sends match the ownership map. If the two systems cannot coordinate the required exclusion, consolidate that recovery stage under one owner before activation.</p>
<h2>Budget for incremental profit, not two revenue dashboards</h2>
<p>Hypothetical incentive example: a $368 basket with a 42% full-price gross margin has $213.44 in fixed product cost and $154.56 gross profit. A 15% discount removes $55.20 from revenue; $312.80 minus $213.44 leaves $99.36 gross profit, before payment, fulfillment, returns and messaging costs. It does not leave $131.38: the discount reduces revenue, not product cost.</p>
<p>Compare the native bill with an Attentive quote covering email, text volume, destination mix, onboarding and any required services. Add staff time and overlap during transition. If an incremental recovered order leaves $20 after variable costs and a proposed program adds $400 per month, it needs 20 additional orders to cover that cost; attributed orders that would have returned anyway do not count as additional.</p>
<p>Use a randomized no-recovery comparison where feasible, with a duration and sample chosen for your traffic. Deduplicate by Shopify order ID and reconcile discounts, refunds and all channel costs. If traffic cannot support a reliable estimate, retain that uncertainty instead of treating platform attribution as proven lift.</p>
<h2>Practical recommendation</h2>
<p>Keep native recovery when it meets the tested workflow and the staff can run it reliably. Consider Attentive when coordinated journey stages and a dedicated email/text program solve a demonstrated operating problem and the quote fits incremental economics. Sequenzy can be evaluated as another supplier, but there is no automatic need for a third orchestration layer: adding one without a verified responsibility creates another source of overlapping sends.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Recovery decision FAQ" />
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
